WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:05.260
We were experimenting with psychedelics as
much as we were playing music.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.440
It's a philosophy of leaving yourself open
to possibility

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.600
and leaving yourself open to magic.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.320
The Acid Test experience really formed the
band as a group mind.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.540
The Grateful Dead.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.460
The band forged during the acid tests of the
1960s,

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.280
that grew to become one of the most popular live
acts in American history,

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:28.380
is the subject of a new four hour film by
academy award nominated director

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.540
Amir Bar-Lev.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.460
There's sex, drugs, rock and roll.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.240
But also a kind of a different attitude towards
fame

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.700
and towards the relationship with fans,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.280
that I think people, whether you like the
band or not,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.480
are going to find informative and interesting.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.019
You know, when I was a teenager picking from
the palette of cultural options out there

00:00:52.019 --> 00:00:53.129
and, you know,

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:58.579
picking from stuff that MTV wanted to serve
me, or exploring the Grateful Dead,

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:05.880
the Grateful Dead represented a kind of a
high standard of honest music.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:06.400
You know?

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.800
That I changed my life and my attitude towards
music and I know I'm not alone.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:20.700
If you were in the audience you were included
in the experience.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.500
We should have known something was coming,

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.500
when there were more people outside the show,

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.640
than there were inside.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.320
I'm a lefty, right. So when I hear that Tucker
Carlson and Anne Coulter

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.280
call themselves Deadheads,

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:38.520
my natural response is to say 'No, they don't
get it.'

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.780
But that's, I think, wrong.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.300
That's the wrong attitude that I have.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.500
And if you look at what the Grateful Dead
were about, it was a radical inclusivity,

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:54.000
and there was no one way to be a Deadhead,
everybody was welcome:

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.110
Motorcyclists, newbies, gatecrashers, psycho
right-wingers.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:03.320
I think they are the musical statue of liberty,
you know?

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.960
Because they invited everybody to participate,
not just to watch, to participate.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.700
There was this strong belief that it be leaderless.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.500
Who was in charge?

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.960
Well I'm so glad you asked that.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.000
There were times when I was in charge, times
when Jerry was in charge,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.859
you had a truck that had a blown carburetor,
that carburetor was the boss.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:27.500
I think Jerry was radically anti-authoritarian
and all the guys are, in the band.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:35.520
And so, you know, when people began to elect
them to be authorities,

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.480
they had a natural concern and skepticism
around that.

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:40.489
And I think that's healthy.

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:47.100
I wish more people in power were concerned
about power and wielding power.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.120
You know, and I think that's what gave the
Grateful Dead it's longevity.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.380
It was never about one person or seven people.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.359
It was something more pluralistic.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.000
The band had a healthy skepticism

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.099
around a kind of top-down authoritarian approach to community.

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:09.680
The Grateful Dead famously let people tape
their music and, you know,

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.299
keep bootlegs of them for free.

00:03:12.299 --> 00:03:16.730
And I think, you know, that was part and parcel
of an ethos with the Grateful Dead,

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:20.220
that, you know, that you trust people.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.740
That made us feel like we were participating
in something and that we were trustworthy.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:28.980
And you know, of course that drew more people
to them.

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:33.299
It was very American because it, you know,
you weren't being told what to do,

00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:36.529
you were being asked to do the right thing

00:03:36.529 --> 00:03:39.920
and being made responsible for your own actions.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.280
It's not up to us to define the Grateful Dead.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.600
It's a living, breathing thing.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.880
I think that's one of the parts of it's magic.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.320
Not defining it is that it becomes everything.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.319
The Grateful Dead had nothing to do with creating
a community around them.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:57.489
They just made space for it, you know?

00:03:57.489 --> 00:04:02.269
They made space by not taking up all the room
with themselves and their own egos.

00:04:02.269 --> 00:04:06.709
That's like so refreshing and missing from
popular culture today.

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:10.260
They, you know, there's this great line in the film
where Jerry says:

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.720
'What I loved about the acid test is people
weren't there to see us.'

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.700
Do you think, you know, would you ever hear
a rock star say:

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.079
'the best thing about those early shows is
we weren't the center of attention.'

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:24.909
It set a tone that, you know, is why they're
still here today.

